    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A concrete repair kit is not one product. The label covers injection kits for leaking basement walls, two-part epoxy patch kits for broken edges, liquid crack seal kits for slab cracks and bagged resurfacing systems for worn surfaces. They share nothing except the word concrete. Buy the kit that matches the damage in front of you and it is a convenient, fairly priced bundle. Buy the wrong one and you will either waste the box or make the next repair harder.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="kit-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Four Kinds of Concrete Repair Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search for a concrete repair kit and the results mix four product families that do not overlap. The first is the crack injection kit, built for poured concrete foundation walls. It contains surface ports, a paste to glue them on and seal the crack face, and a cartridge of injection resin, either a rigid epoxy or an expanding polyurethane. These kits exist to push resin through the full thickness of a wall, typically 8 to 10 inches, and they are the only kit type that genuinely does that.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second family is the two-part epoxy patch kit. These are small, usually a pair of tubs or a dual cartridge of thick epoxy putty or mortar, sometimes with a bag of graded sand or aggregate. They are meant for rebuilding broken step noses, chipped slab edges, anchor holes and small spalls where the repair has to stick hard to old concrete and carry some impact. They are strong, fast and expensive per cubic inch, which is why they come in small quantities.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third family is the crack seal kit for slabs, sold for driveways, walkways, patios and garage floors. The box usually holds a squeeze bottle or caulk tube of acrylic, latex or polyurethane sealant, sometimes a length of foam backer rod, and occasionally a wire brush or nozzle set. These are the most common kits on the shelf and the most misunderstood, because they seal cracks against water and debris but do nothing to hold concrete together.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fourth family is the resurfacing kit, which is really a bagged polymer-modified cement plus a squeegee, a broom or a roller. It addresses scaled, pitted and worn surfaces rather than cracks, and it is the largest and heaviest of the four. Some brands also sell bundled patio or driveway restoration kits that combine a crack filler with a resurfacer and a sealer. Those are convenient if you need all three steps, and poor value if you only need one.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Kit type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical contents</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Built for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Not for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crack injection kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ports, surface paste, injection cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poured foundation wall cracks, dry or leaking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slabs, block walls, wide gaps</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy patch kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two-part epoxy putty or mortar, sometimes sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Step noses, edges, anchor holes, small spalls</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large areas, moving cracks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crack seal kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealant tube or bottle, backer rod, brush</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slab cracks on drives, walks, patios</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Structural bonding, wall leaks under pressure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Resurfacing kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymer cement, squeegee or broom</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scaled or pitted slabs that are still sound</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Active cracks, heaved or sunken slabs</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Before you compare brands, decide which of these four you actually need. Most bad kit purchases come from a homeowner with a moving driveway crack who buys an epoxy patch kit because it sounds stronger, or a homeowner with a leaking wall crack who buys a slab sealant because it was on the endcap. The rest of this guide walks through each family so you can make that call in about a minute.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="in-the-box" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Is Actually in the Box
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical foundation crack injection kit is sized for one crack of roughly 8 to 10 feet in a standard wall. Expect around a dozen plastic surface ports, a two-part epoxy paste for bonding the ports and capping the crack, one or two cartridges of injection resin, and a mixing nozzle. Some kits include a manual caulking gun adapter, a small spatula and gloves. Brands such as RadonSeal sell both epoxy and polyurethane versions of this format, and the contents are broadly similar across the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the resin type before you buy. Epoxy injection resin is low viscosity and cures rigid, so it bonds the two faces of a dormant crack back together. Polyurethane injection resin reacts with water, expands into a flexible foam and seals leaks, but adds no meaningful strength. A kit labeled only as crack repair may contain either. If the description does not say which resin is in the cartridge, assume the seller does not want you to know and look elsewhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy patch kits are simpler. You get resin and hardener, either as two tubs you knead or stir together, or as a side-by-side cartridge with a static mixing nozzle. Manufacturers usually quote a working time of a few minutes to around 20 minutes depending on temperature, and the cure to full strength runs from several hours to a day. Coverage is the catch: a kit might fill a few dozen cubic inches, which is one broken step corner, not a run of damaged steps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crack seal kits for slabs contain the least. Often it is a single 10 fluid ounce tube or a squeeze bottle, which manufacturers rate for a set number of linear feet at a given crack size. As a rough guide, a standard tube runs out fast in cracks wider than 1/4 inch unless backer rod or sand takes up most of the depth. Kits that include backer rod are worth seeking out, because the rod is the part most homeowners skip and the part that most affects how long the repair lasts.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">General Reference Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-10 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Crack length a single injection kit usually targets</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Crack width where backer rod usually becomes necessary</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/4-1/2 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical sealant depth in a slab joint</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common minimum application temperature on labels</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Resurfacing kits are mostly cement. A bag or two of polymer-modified resurfacer, a long-handled squeegee and sometimes a broom for a non-slip texture. Coverage depends almost entirely on thickness, and manufacturers usually rate a bag for a given square footage at about 1/8 inch. Applied thicker to fill pitting, the same bag covers far less. Read the coverage chart against your square footage before buying, and plan on one extra bag rather than running short halfway across a slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="matching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Matching the Concrete Crack Repair Kit to the Damage
          </h2>

          <p>
            The question that decides which concrete crack repair kit you need is not how wide the crack is. It is whether the crack is in a wall or a slab, whether it leaks, and whether it is still moving. A hairline crack in a basement wall that seeps after rain needs an injection kit. A hairline crack in a driveway that never leaks needs, at most, a cheap liquid seal. Same width, completely different product. Start with location and behavior, then use width to pick the size of the repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            To check movement, mark both ends of the crack with a pencil and note the date, then glue a thin strip of tape or a dab of plaster across it. If the crack lengthens or the plaster breaks over a few weeks or a season, it is active. Active cracks need a flexible product, either polyurethane injection in a wall or a polyurethane sealant in a slab. Rigid epoxy across a crack that is still moving simply cracks again next to the repair.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Damage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Right kit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leaking crack in a poured wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane injection kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Foam seals the full wall depth and flexes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dry, dormant crack in a poured wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy injection kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rebonds the crack; skip if it never leaks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hairline slab cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Liquid crack seal kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin liquid wicks in and blocks water</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Moving slab crack 1/4 in or wider</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane sealant plus backer rod</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexes with the slab through seasons</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Broken step nose or slab edge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two-part epoxy patch kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bonds hard and takes impact</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scaled or pitted surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Resurfacing kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Restores a uniform wearing surface</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            There are also cases where no kit is the right answer. A horizontal crack in a basement wall, a wall that bows inward, stair-step cracking in block, or any crack wider than about 1/4 inch with one side offset from the other is a structural signal. Injecting it hides the evidence without addressing the soil pressure or settlement behind it. Our guide to <a href="/foundation-crack-repair-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">foundation crack repair cost</a> covers what an engineer and a repair contractor typically charge for that kind of problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Block walls are the other common mismatch. Injection kits are designed for solid poured concrete. Pump resin into a crack in a hollow block wall and much of it disappears into the cores, which is why block wall cracks are usually repaired from the surface with a patch, sealant or coating. If your basement walls are block, look at our guide on <a href="/caulk-for-basement-wall-cracks/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">caulk for basement wall cracks</a> and the broader <a href="/basement-crack-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement crack sealer</a> breakdown instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="injection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Using an Epoxy or Polyurethane Injection Kit, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by cleaning the crack face. Scrape off paint, efflorescence and loose material with a wire brush for about an inch on each side of the crack. Ports and surface paste will not bond to paint or white mineral bloom, and a single poorly bonded port is where resin will blow out under pressure. If the crack is actively running water, most kits tell you to wait for a drier day or to use the polyurethane version, which tolerates and even needs some moisture to react.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, place the ports. The common rule printed in kit instructions is a port every 8 to 12 inches along the crack, with one near the bottom and one near the top. Thinner walls and tighter cracks get closer spacing. Each port is set directly over the crack using a dab of the epoxy paste, and some kits use a small nail or finishing wire through the port to keep the crack opening clear of paste while you set it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then cap the crack. Spread the surface paste over the entire crack between the ports, about 1/8 inch thick and roughly an inch wide, feathering it out onto the wall. This seal is what forces the resin into the wall instead of out of the face. Let the paste cure for the time on the label, often 30 minutes to a few hours depending on temperature. Injecting into soft paste is the most common reason a kit fails on the first attempt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inject from the lowest port upward. Push resin into the bottom port slowly until it appears at the next port up, then cap the bottom port and move the gun to the next one. Go slowly. Low, steady pressure gives the resin time to travel through the full wall thickness, and kit instructions consistently warn that forcing it fast only fills the outer inch. On a tight hairline crack the cartridge may barely move for several minutes per port, which is normal.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE INJECTION RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Bottom to top, slow and steady, and never into wet paste.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Resin should travel up the crack and show at the next port before you move. If it squirts out of the face, the surface seal failed there; patch it, let it cure, and continue.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Once the resin has cured, which manufacturers typically rate at a day or so for epoxy and less for most polyurethane foams, cut or knock the ports off flush and grind or scrape the surface paste if you plan to paint or finish the wall. Leave it on an unfinished basement wall if you like; it does no harm. Check the outside of the wall after the next heavy rain. A dry crack line means the resin reached the exterior face.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="slab-kits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Using a Patch or Crack Seal Kit on Slabs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Slab crack seal kits work only as well as the cleaning before them. Dig out dirt, weeds and old sealant with a screwdriver or a crack chaser, then blow the crack out with a leaf blower, compressed air or a shop vacuum. If the crack is narrower than about 1/8 inch, many installers widen it slightly with an angle grinder and a diamond blade so the sealant has somewhere to go. The surface must be dry, and most labels ask for air and slab temperatures above roughly 40 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cracks wider than about 1/4 inch, push closed-cell foam backer rod into the crack first so the sealant depth ends up roughly half the crack width, typically 1/4 to 1/2 inch. This matters because a sealant bonded to the bottom of the crack as well as both sides cannot stretch, and it tears within a season. Fill deep cracks below the rod with dry sand if the kit did not include enough rod. Our tutorial on <a href="/sealing-cracks-in-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">sealing cracks in a driveway</a> covers the tooling in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the sealant slightly below the surface, not proud of it. A bead that sits above the slab gets scuffed by tires, shovels and feet. Self-leveling products flow flat on their own on a level slab; non-sag products need tooling with a wet finger, a spoon or a plastic tool. Keep traffic off for the time on the label, which is often a day for foot traffic and longer for vehicles. Cool, damp weather extends polyurethane cure times noticeably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy patch kits for broken edges and steps start with sawing or chiseling the damaged area back to sound concrete, ideally with square shoulders rather than a feathered edge. Clean and dampen the area if the label calls for it, then mix the two parts completely until the color is uniform. Press the mortar hard into the repair to drive out air, build up in layers if the kit limits depth, and shape the step nose with a trowel or a temporary form board. Work fast; warm days shorten pot life considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            One honest warning about epoxy patch kits on outdoor slabs. Epoxy expands and contracts at a different rate from concrete, and a large epoxy patch in direct sun can debond around its edges over a few seasons. For anything larger than a few inches across outdoors, a polymer-modified cement patch is often the more durable and far cheaper choice. Save epoxy for small, high-stress spots like anchor holes, step corners and the lip of a garage apron.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resurfacing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Resurfacing Kits and Their Limits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Resurfacing kits are the right buy when the slab is structurally fine but the surface looks bad: scaling from deicers, pitting, popouts across the top, or a rough and worn finish. They put down a thin new wearing layer, usually 1/16 to 1/2 inch thick depending on the product, bonded to the old concrete. Done well, the slab looks new for years. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete resurfacers</a> compares the main bagged products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resurfacer will not fix cracks. A thin overlay bonded over an active crack will crack in the same place, usually within the first winter. That is called reflective cracking, and it is the most common complaint owners report about resurfacing projects. The fix is to treat cracks first with a flexible sealant, and to carry control joints through the new layer by cutting or taping them so the overlay can move where the slab moves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Preparation is where resurfacing kits succeed or fail. Manufacturers typically require a pressure wash at around 3,000 psi or higher to remove dirt, sealers and weak surface material, then a saturated surface dry condition: damp but with no standing water. Oil stains must be degreased. A previously sealed slab may need grinding or chemical stripping, since resurfacer will not bond to a film-forming sealer. Skipping any of these is the usual cause of delamination.
          </p>

          <p>
            Working time is short. Most resurfacers give you around 20 minutes in moderate temperatures, less in heat, which is why manufacturers recommend working in sections of roughly 100 to 150 square feet bounded by control joints. Mix small batches, pour, squeegee, broom and move on. Two people make this far easier. If you are covering a full two-car driveway, a resurfacing kit is often not enough material, and buying bags by the pallet becomes the only practical route.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some resurfacing kits are sold as patio or driveway restoration systems that include a crack sealant and a top sealer. Look at the quantities before paying the premium. A single small tube of crack filler rarely covers the cracks on a real driveway, and the included sealer may be a low-solids acrylic you would not choose on its own. Often it is cheaper to buy the resurfacer by the bag and pick the crack sealant and sealer separately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kit-or-parts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Buying Components Separately Beats a Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kits make sense when the quantities match your job and you would not otherwise know what to buy. A foundation injection kit is the clearest example. The ports, the paste and the resin are designed to work together, and buying them as separate contractor items usually costs more for a single crack, not less. If you have one or two wall cracks, buy the kit and follow its instructions exactly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Loose components win almost everywhere else. For slab cracks, a single tube of a well-rated polyurethane sealant and a roll of backer rod usually cost less than a branded crack repair kit and give you more of the parts that matter. For broken steps, a bag of polymer-modified patching cement repairs several corners for the price of one small epoxy kit. For resurfacing, bags bought by quantity are cheaper per square foot than a kit with a squeegee you may already own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other reason to buy components is choice. A kit locks you into one sealant, one patch and one coating, chosen partly to hit a price point. Buying separately lets you choose a polyurethane sealant for a moving driveway crack, a liquid seal for hairlines in the same slab, and a penetrating sealer to protect the whole surface afterward. Our pillar on the <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete crack filler</a> ranks the individual products by the crack they suit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of kits sold mainly through social media ads with a single tube of unbranded waterproof glue and dramatic before-and-after photos. Those listings rarely name the chemistry, the coverage or the temperature range, which are the three facts you need. Our look at <a href="/magic-crack-filler-reviews/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">magic crack filler reviews</a> covers what buyers commonly report about that category and what to buy instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest rule: buy a kit for wall injection, buy components for slab cracks, buy the patching material you need for edges and steps, and buy resurfacer by the bag. If you are still unsure which category your damage falls into, read our broader guide to <a href="/concrete-repair-products/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair products</a> first, then come back and choose the box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an epoxy kit for a moving crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy cures rigid and bonds the two sides of a crack together. If the slab or wall is still moving through seasons, the crack simply opens again next to the epoxy. Check for movement first, and use a flexible polyurethane product on any crack that is still active or reopens each winter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Injecting a block wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Injection kits are built for solid poured concrete. Pumped into a crack in a hollow block wall, the resin mostly drains into the cores and never seals the crack face. Block wall cracks are usually repaired from the surface with patching cement, flexible sealant or a coating, and stair-step cracking deserves a structural check first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping backer rod in wide slab cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sealant bonded to the bottom of a deep crack as well as both sides cannot stretch, so it tears within a season. Backer rod sized slightly larger than the crack keeps sealant depth to about half the width. It costs very little and is the most commonly skipped step in every slab crack kit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Injecting before the surface paste cures</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The paste that holds the ports and caps the crack has to cure before any pressure goes in. Inject too soon and resin blows out of the crack face, wasting the cartridge and coating the wall. Wait the full time on the label, longer in a cold basement, and test with gentle pressure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resurfacing over unsealed cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thin resurfacing layer bonded across an active crack cracks in the same place within a winter or two. Treat cracks first with a flexible sealant, keep existing control joints open through the new layer, and expect any untreated crack to reappear. Resurfacer fixes surfaces, not movement.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best concrete repair kit for a leaking basement wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a leaking crack in a poured concrete wall, a polyurethane injection kit is the usual DIY choice. The resin reacts with water, expands to fill the crack through the wall thickness and stays flexible. Epoxy kits are better for dry, dormant cracks where you want strength. Neither is suitable for block walls or for horizontal or bowing cracks, which need a structural assessment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do concrete crack repair kits work on driveways?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slab crack seal kits work on driveways if the crack is cleaned properly, backer rod fills deeper cracks and the temperature is within the label range. They seal out water and debris, which slows further damage, but they do not glue the slab back together. For cracks that move with the seasons, a polyurethane sealant tends to last longer than acrylic or latex fillers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an injection kit take from start to finish?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on most of a day of elapsed time but only a couple of hours of work. Cleaning and setting ports takes under an hour, the surface paste usually needs 30 minutes to a few hours to cure, and injecting a single crack can take an hour if it is tight. The resin then cures for roughly a day before ports are trimmed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an epoxy patch kit on a large spalled area?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is usually the wrong tool. Epoxy patch kits come in small quantities, cost a lot per cubic inch and expand differently from concrete in sun and frost. For spalled areas more than a few inches across, a polymer-modified cement patch or a resurfacer is cheaper and tends to last better. Keep epoxy for small, high-stress spots like anchor holes and step corners.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature do concrete repair kits need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most kit labels specify minimum air and surface temperatures of roughly 40 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, rising during cure. Cold slows curing sharply, especially for epoxy, and can leave resin soft for days. Heat shortens working time, so on hot days work in shade or early morning. Always check the specific label, because ranges vary by chemistry and brand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a resurfacing kit enough for a whole driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Resurfacing kits are sized for patios, walks and small slabs, and a typical two-car driveway needs many bags at 1/8 inch, more if you are filling pitting. For a full driveway, buy resurfacer by the bag or pallet, line up a second person, and work in sections between control joints so each batch stays workable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should I skip the kit and call a professional?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Call a professional for horizontal basement wall cracks, walls that bow or lean, stair-step cracking in block, cracks wider than about 1/4 inch with one side offset, and slabs that have heaved or sunk noticeably. Those point to soil pressure or settlement. A kit can seal the symptom, but it will not address the cause, and it can hide evidence an engineer would want to see.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

